{"pdfpcFormat":2,"disableMarkdown":false,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":1,"label":"1","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Memory corruption → control of the program counter. \n- W⊕X stops injected shellcode, so attackers *reuse* existing code: gadgets. \n- Walk the figure: execve(\"/bin/sh\") is the classic chain. It stages the string in writable memory, loads the three syscall arguments, then issues the syscall: one C call, seven gadgets. \n- It was long the sole focus of automated tools: shell is easy to program, execve leaves no crash behind, and \"/bin/sh\\0\" fits in one register. \n- Shacham (2007): ROP is Turing-complete."},{"idx":2,"label":"2","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Four challenges, taken from ch1 §Problem Statement. \n- The tools people actually use are the oldest and simplest. Hard chains are still built by hand. \n- Almost all research covers single-path programs only."},{"idx":3,"label":"3","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- Thesis (ch1, after Aho and Ullman): CS is finding the right model for a problem and mechanisable techniques to solve it. Ghidra's p-code is that model for code-reuse synthesis. \n- Two mechanised techniques built on it, one per paper: conflict-driven (CRACKERS) and goal-directed (Simulac-ROP). \n- The colour coding (blue = Paper 1, plum = Paper 2) is used for the rest of the talk."},{"idx":4,"label":"4","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":5,"label":"5","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Ghidra's SLEIGH specs lift machine code for dozens of ISAs into one register-transfer language. \n- A single semantics means one tool covers every architecture. \n- The fine-grained RTL gives algorithm design plenty of room. Both papers build on it."},{"idx":6,"label":"6","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 2 min\n  \n- The encoding is over QF_ABV: bitvectors plus arrays. Each p-code space (register, ram, unique) is a byte-addressed array. \n- Four layers (ch3): *state*, *read* a varnode out of a state, *operation* semantics per opcode (state-free), *write* a value back into a state. \n- (a)/(b): conflict-driven synthesis composes all four, one state per p-code op, so the final state is a term over the initial one. \n- (c): goal-directed synthesis uses only the operation layer, applied to terms from a dataflow summary. Both approaches share one definition of what each opcode means. \n- Mechanised from SLEIGH (30+ architectures in Ghidra) and released as the `jingle` library."},{"idx":7,"label":"7","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 2.5 min\n  \n- Lineage: SGC encoded the whole problem as one SMT query, which did not scale. CRACKERS splits it: SAT selects gadgets, SMT validates the chain, and the UNSAT core feeds back. \n- Inputs: the binary, a linear p-code *reference program*, and specification constraints (pre/postconditions, pointer invariants). \n- The reference program is cut into *steps*, and partitions are tried shortest-first. N steps give exactly N gadgets. \n- Each step gets a candidate pool. The outer SAT problem picks one per step; the inner SMT problem checks the chain model. \n- On UNSAT, the core becomes a conflict clause. When no selection remains, move on to the next partition. \n- Threat model: no assumed memory layout. Anything not constrained is free for the solver. That is the key contrast with Part II."},{"idx":8,"label":"8","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Benchmark: 1000 Debian x86-64 binaries against the open-source tools. \n- Best success rate by a wide margin and the fastest median time per kB. \n- On the SGC corpus it matched SGC, failing only where no gadget writes r8/r9. \n- Runtime scales linearly with the number of candidates and exponentially with chain length, which motivates Paper 2."},{"idx":9,"label":"9","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- This bridge slide is the motivation for Part II (ch4 §Limitations, ch6 intro). \n- Specs are linear reference programs, so the user fixes the shape *and order* of the chain; any solution in a different order is out of reach. \n- One gadget per step: every gadget must make forward progress, so there are no setup gadgets. Linear, single-block gadgets only. \n- Conflict clauses are coarse: one proposition per gadget, so nothing generalises to similar gadgets (no theory propagation). Under realistic constraints the loop degrades towards enumeration. \n- It can't reason about entry/exit values, i.e. whether inputs are preserved. \n- The Paper 2 benchmark reran crackers: many generated chains were *invalid* under strict memory checks."},{"idx":10,"label":"10","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":11,"label":"11","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Same threat model as ropbot (ch6 §A Tightened Threat Model): known library base, a contiguous attacker-controlled region holding the chain at an *unknown* address, and a way to dispatch into it. \n- Contrast with CRACKERS: there, every unconstrained bit of state is assumed controlled, and the user must describe the memory model. In practice the running example pins RSP, which already assumes the chain's location is known. \n- Given up: other layouts. The chain lives in one region addressed by vPC, and every branch destination comes from inside it. \n- Gained: realism (the side-effect policy is fixed by the threat model, with no per-target configuration) and scalability (patches compose locally, so there is no whole-chain SMT model during search)."},{"idx":12,"label":"12","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 2 min\n  \n- Gadget analysis is an abstract interpretation over p-code in a Configurable Program Analysis (CPA). Multi-block, loop-free gadgets get one effect summary Φ; two controllable exits make a *guard gadget*. \n- The spec is a goal set G in SpIL, not a reference program: the user states *what*, not the chain's shape. \n- Walk the loop: pop a state (G, C); for each gadget compute the residual of G under Φ. Trivial residual → a patch candidate, concretised by SMT. Otherwise push it, unless the frontier already holds a dominating state (antichain pruning). \n- Backwards: the first gadget found executes last. \n- Contrast with Part I: a failed concretisation is just dropped. Nothing is learned from it."},{"idx":13,"label":"13","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- A patch is the region of memory from vPC to vPC+n. Its tuple is size, first gadget, constraints and destination. The idea comes from ropbot and is extended here. \n- Residuals form a small algebra (ch6 §Residuals and Goal Matching): match a value, match a location, merge with ⊔. Merging two residuals can fail, which prunes the search. \n- Constraints are equalities over SPIL values rooted in patch bytes, so conflicts are detected syntactically. \n- A residual is the goal obligations that remain plus the patch constraints an effect induces. It is a tractable approximation of the weakest precondition. \n- Example: goal rax := 5 with effect rax ↦ entry(rcx) leaves the residual rcx := 5. With effect rax ↦ [rsp+0], the goal is discharged into a patch constraint."},{"idx":14,"label":"14","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Benchmark adopted from ropbot (current state of the art): 48 cores, 30-minute timeout, angr-validated. \n- Simulac-ROP has the most verified chains on x64 facefeed and mmap, and is level on execve and dup. \n- Compared with Paper 1 on the same benchmark: crackers verified 141 of 347 generated chains. Simulac-ROP verified 645. \n- Chain build is about 30× faster than ropbot (median 0.1 s vs 3.1 s on facefeed). \n- Multi-arch: mipsel 358 vs 287, aarch64 tied, riscv 343 vs 360."},{"idx":15,"label":"15","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Chains are checked *independently* of the synthesiser by angr symbolic execution, with strict memory permissions. \n- The three outcomes are Verified, Invalid (semantic or memory-permission violation) and Unknown (angr cannot execute the chain). \n- Linear Simulac-ROP had zero invalid chains on x64 and a handful elsewhere. Causes: edge cases in the gadget CPA and p-code semantic errors. \n- TODO: say what verification contributes beyond the paper version."},{"idx":16,"label":"16","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 2 min\n  \n- A guarded chain has the form \"if f(v) then C₁ else C₂\", Dijkstra-style. Arms may nest. \n- Use cases: failure handling (mmap returns −1 → exit), targeting (geteuid == 0 → shell). \n- Steps: synthesise a guard prefix ending in a guard gadget, schedule the patches so offsets don't conflict, pad both arms to an equal vPC shift, then concatenate and merge. \n- Guard gadgets occur naturally, e.g. in `__libc_csu_init` function-pointer dispatch. \n- No other tool can even *express* these specs. \n- wget CVE-2017-13089 case study: drops a root shell when running as root, prints a message otherwise."},{"idx":17,"label":"17","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- Paper version: angr is run once per spec path, with return values set for that path. \n- A reviewer found a hole. On x64, a guard testing 2·x = 0 also accepts x = 0x80000000, and per-path concrete runs never exercise that input. \n- Revalidation with the stricter checker turned most x64 guarded chains from verified to invalid (next slide). \n- This is the direct motivation for the current work. Give the summary here; details go in Part III."},{"idx":18,"label":"18","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- The numbers are from the 17 Oct tables in simulacrop_paper/tables. \n- mmap and the non-x64 architectures survived revalidation. The x64 facefeed, execve and dup guarded chains did not. \n- Three nested guards (g.dup+root): 20 of 32 time out. Guard selection and padding merging scale poorly. \n- Say plainly that fixing this is the current work."},{"idx":19,"label":"19","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false},{"idx":20,"label":"20","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Frame this as a direct response to the reviewers: soundness, composition, related work. \n- TODO: fill in what is actually in progress and how far along it is."},{"idx":21,"label":"21","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- Paper 1 is published at USENIX Security '25, with artifacts available (jingle and crackers are open source). \n- Paper 2's history: S&P '27 and USENIX '27 both rejected. Be candid; the reviews are constructive and specific. \n- TODO: target venue and deadline, and whether there is a third paper or a workshop paper."},{"idx":22,"label":"22","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Title: \"Automated Synthesis of Code-Reuse Attacks from Branching-Time Specifications\". The thesis is in LaTeX on the OxThesis template. \n- Since 17 Nov: ch3's SMT encoding section is written, ch4 (conflict-driven) has its algorithm ported from Paper 1, and ch6 (goal-directed) has the Paper 2 text ported. New figures (synthesis loop, search loop, encoding layers) are shared with this talk. \n- Still open in ch3: §Analysis of P-Code (the CPA), which ch6 depends on. Ch4 and ch6 still need threat-model, correctness and limitations prose. \n- Ch5 and ch7–9 are skeletons, but each maps onto a written paper, so the work is porting and expanding rather than writing new. \n- Word counts are from the .tex sources on 22 Nov 2026 (detex) and are approximate."},{"idx":23,"label":"23","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1.5 min\n  \n- Submission target from thesis.tex: Hilary 2027. This is a draft. Adjust the bars in the `tasks` array. \n- The critical path is guarded verification → Paper 2 resubmission → ch7–8. \n- Ch4 and ch6 are ported; finishing them (limitations, correctness) and ch3's CPA section can run in parallel with the verification work. \n- TODO: confirm every date. If the Hilary 2027 submission is too tight, say so here."},{"idx":24,"label":"24","overlay":0,"forcedOverlay":false,"hidden":false,"note":"⏱ 1 min\n  \n- Recap the arc: the foundation in p-code, then conflict-driven (Paper 1), then goal-directed and guarded (Paper 2), then verification. \n- Thesis draft: ≈19k words, both algorithm chapters ported. \n- Close on the plan and invite questions."}]}
